33:["$","$L56",null,{"children":[["$","$L57",null,{"title":"`InvalidArgument: Expected 1 key in file`","id":"invalidargument-expected-1-key-in-file","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["The key file holds more than one key (a file with none fails earlier, with\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"Could not extract key(s) from file"}],"). A concatenated bundle is the usual cause.\nSplit it, and leave the chain in ",["$","code",null,{"children":"cert_file"}]," where multiple PEM blocks are fine."]}]}],["$","$L57",null,{"title":"Clients get connection errors and the server logs `Failed to accept tls stream`","id":"clients-hang-instead-of-failing","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Almost always ",["$","code",null,{"children":"grpc://"}]," against a TLS listener. The client sends plaintext HTTP/2\ninto a socket waiting for a handshake, the handshake fails, and the server drops\nthe connection. Check the scheme everywhere: Bazel flags, ",["$","code",null,{"children":"grpc"}]," store\naddresses, ",["$","code",null,{"children":"worker_api_endpoint.uri"}],", and any healthcheck."]}]}],["$","$L57",null,{"title":"The client rejects the server certificate","id":"certificate-signature-verify-failed-from-a-client","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["The client does not trust the CA that signed the server certificate. Point it at\nthe right ",["$","code",null,{"children":"ca_file"}],", or ",["$","code",null,{"children":"--tls_certificate"}]," for Bazel. When the client is\nForge itself (a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"grpc"}]," store or a worker) the error reads ",["$","code",null,{"children":"invalid peer certificate: UnknownIssuer"}],". If the server certificate was issued by an\nintermediate, ",["$","code",null,{"children":"cert_file"}]," must contain the full chain, leaf first, because\nclients will not fetch a missing intermediate for you."]}]}],["$","$L57",null,{"title":"`peer sent no certificates` after adding `client_ca_file`","id":"peer-did-not-return-a-certificate-after-adding-client_ca_file","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["The listener now requires client certificates and this client has none. The\nserver logs it as ",["$","code",null,{"children":"Failed to accept tls stream"}]," with that reason, and the client\nsees the handshake aborted. That includes clients you may have forgotten are\nclients: workers dialing the scheduler, ",["$","code",null,{"children":"grpc"}]," stores, and anything probing a\nhealth endpoint."]}]}],["$","$L57",null,{"title":"Certificates renewed but the old ones are still in use","id":"certificates-renewed-but-the-old-ones-are-still-in-use","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Server certificates and a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"grpc"}]," store's client certificates are read once at\nstartup, and a worker re-reads its client files only when it reconnects to the\nscheduler. Renewal means restarting the process. Plan for it, because a\ncert-manager rotation that nothing restarts will quietly expire."]}]}],["$","$L57",null,{"title":"How do I give different teams different access?","id":"how-do-i-give-different-teams-different-access","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"You can't, inside Forge. It has no per-caller permissions. The\napproaches that work are running separate instances with separate stores for\nseparate trust domains, or putting an authorizing proxy in front and letting\nForge trust everything the proxy passes through. If you take the second\nroute, the worker API must not be behind the same proxy."}]}]]}]
